{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfs78rbiHa3fvgXVimatV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the one-cent mismatch in `invoice_total` in `billing/totals.py`. The full suite passes (5 tests, including two new ones).\n\n**Root cause:** The PDF prints each line rounded to cents, but `invoice_total` computed the gross amount and the unrounded discounts separately and rounded once at the end. The sum of rounded lines and the rounded sum of unrounded values can differ by a cent. That only happens with percentage discounts that leave a fractional cent, which explains the \"discounts only, about 1 in 20 invoices\" pattern. Nothing raises an error, so there was nothing in the logs.\n\n**Fix:** `invoice_total` now sums `line_totals(inv)`, the same rounded amounts the PDF shows, and then adds tax on that sum. The public API is unchanged.\n\n**Regression tests:** I added two to `tests/test_billing.py`. They assert that `invoice_total` equals `sum(line_totals)` for invoices with half-cent discounts. Before the fix both failed, and with the fix both pass.\n\n**Tax:** Tax is now computed on the sum of the rounded lines, so invoices with both discounts and tax can change by a cent compared with before. That is the intended behaviour, since the total now matches the printed lines, but you may want finance to confirm it.\n\n**Tracked `.pyc` files:** The repo tracks `.pyc` files, and running the tests modified them. They show as modified in `git status`, and I didn't commit anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1459,"cache_read_input_tokens":35138,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1459},"output_tokens":491,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"ses